#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222376} :activity/id #uuid "80e6356e-9b75-58b6-9da9-f8c9047c131d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048443 :topic/id #uuid "9fcc2c1e-7bc0-5b8e-a825-9ff84b01fab5" :topic/math-academy-id 6382} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222376 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222514 :tutorial/content "When a function has period $2L$ (instead of $2\\pi$), the \"usual\" Fourier formulas still work, but the sine and cosine inputs must be rescaled so that each term completes an integer number of oscillations over one period. \n\nWe do this by converting the $2L$-periodic function into a $2\\pi$-periodic one using a change of variables.\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in[-L,L)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\nTo use the standard $2\\pi$-period Fourier formulas, define\n\n$$\ns=\\frac{\\pi}{L}x\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ng(s)=f\\!\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi}s\\right).\n$$\n\nThen $g$ is $2\\pi$-periodic.\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $g$, the Fourier series is\n\n$$\ng(s)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\bigl(a_n\\cos(ns)+b_n\\sin(ns)\\bigr),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,ds, \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds,\\,n\\ge 1, \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,ds,\\,n\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $g(s)=f\\!\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right)$ and $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x$.\nFirst, for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\,(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f\\,(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)cos\\,(n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,d\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCoefficients $b_n$ (for $n \\geq 1$) and $a_0$ can be found similarly.\n\nTo summarize, the Fourier series of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(a_n\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the coefficients are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,dx \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "5bb98db0-8e34-5cf2-8911-56a6971740ab" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14681 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0045b15e-7a1f-5142-94c5-082ec814b34d" :step/math-academy-id 601031 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222377}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222377 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221443 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221196 :question/id #uuid "b8658f47-7f42-530a-a62e-1cc3144059aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-21835"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ff3452-19b6-566a-8b39-ad880dee8858" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050676}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550020}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Effects of the Horizontal Scaling on Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "0b4fe87b-2102-5820-bc57-996b33b5332d" :step/math-academy-id 601032 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222378}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222378 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221444 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221197 :question/id #uuid "ba1cc59e-66ed-58fc-a494-b949c7ed0a88" :question/math-academy-id "e-21836"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb301102-1a82-5a81-bb11-b9fc56cd1dc7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549993}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term For the Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "c1a6a016-3b6c-50d2-9290-1aa451df4a51" :step/math-academy-id 601035 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222379}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222379 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221445 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221198 :question/id #uuid "823fc00b-48af-5e79-9dcc-5e519e81cd4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21837"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3315887-ff5c-5fd7-93bd-a58800e378b8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550007}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Coefficients For the Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "b7ee00e8-fdc1-5440-bb38-f920d76832a4" :step/math-academy-id 601038}] :activity/title "Fourier Series of Arbitrary Period" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221473}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525083} :activity/id #uuid "594dca3c-3a9c-5fcb-ade7-ee04621be91e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048443 :topic/id #uuid "9fcc2c1e-7bc0-5b8e-a825-9ff84b01fab5" :topic/math-academy-id 6382} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9caddf0-8911-56aa-9169-d2dd07b18d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{5\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524678} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fefa1eb-6b94-56ab-91b1-2a7b190c1a7a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d8c14d18-5531-531f-b86c-3260af1f5733" :question/math-academy-id "q-340855" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=6x+1$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ The Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{5}.$\n\n${b}_{5}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and $f(x)=6x+1$ for $x\\in (-3,3).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{5}=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(6x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x+1\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{3}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{3}(uv\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{3}{5\\pi }{[(6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(-{[(6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}+6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(-((6(3)+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi )-(6(-3)+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5\\pi ))+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(36+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-5\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\cdot 36 \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2f23af18-6fc6-51a2-9ae3-01f862c5c75a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b5be5de-ac24-5096-b91d-e0e0ff8e3983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372793}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372793} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cb42d37-67e0-5792-9f61-4d038363d4ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7cfbfb37-8e3e-5c3d-8267-64ce726f2c24" :question/math-academy-id "q-341788" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=4{x}^{2}-6x+3$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ The Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-6x+3$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(4{x}^{2}-6x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+3x]}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {3}^{3}-3\\cdot {3}^{2}+3\\cdot 3)-(\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot (-3)^{3}-3\\cdot (-3)^{2}+3\\cdot (-3))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(18-(-72)) \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3a9f9a3f-bf20-54e7-9f47-aa598fef0e88" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4377e0c6-ff5d-58c7-b0be-e00480e92ef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372799} {:answer/id #uuid "e65592e2-0cd5-534f-88d7-0eb0fc781f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi^2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524786}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524786} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b7b647f-bcd2-53cf-8319-b5595a4ddf84" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0b41ae3b-0d3e-5b1e-a78c-fa1ec34fc5b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-341794" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}+4x$ for $x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ The Fourier series of the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+4x$ for $x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(3{x}^{2}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }((\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{8}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2})-(-\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{8}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8a15da49-604b-5a13-b749-afcedfa2e089" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525121}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525121 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d8018e4-ef90-524c-aab7-e82fc5dc5201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524561}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524561} :answer-field/id #uuid "960bde39-7017-5d3d-adb2-123ce9006d52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "53537d13-56ed-578b-89f6-12be53184c9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-341808" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{9}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{9}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3x+\\frac{1}{2}$ for $x\\in [-9,9).$ The Fourier series of the $18$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=9$ and $f(x)=3x+\\frac{1}{2}$ for $x\\in [-9,9).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{-9}^{9}(3x+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x]}_{-9}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}((\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {9}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9)-(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-9)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-9))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(126-117) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a7e42cee-7619-57a2-a6a6-5a997e4e2632" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525130}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525130 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93a95dfd-61c1-5b85-ad7a-7edfc0702959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524618}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524618} :answer-field/id #uuid "79cdb461-38af-5784-a292-e83653dfa433" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6138bd37-a9a0-5af4-80eb-ad7aa981c99c" :question/math-academy-id "q-341815" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & x\\in [0,3) \\\\ -4, & x\\in [-3,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{2}.$\n\n${a}_{2}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3.$ Also, $f(x)=1$ for $x\\in [0,3)$ and $f(x)=-4$ for $x\\in [-3,0).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}(-4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{0}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}x)]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi ))+\\frac{1}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cf1e87c7-8f9a-5050-a189-bceb0d6f5b0e"}] :activity/title "Fourier Series of Arbitrary Period" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524366}]}
